ouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"is considered\"}}]\n35:[\"$\",\"$L2a\",\"child-2075\",{\"child\":\"$23:props:children:0:props:children:props:page:child_items:4:children:5\",\"hasId\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"em-mb-4 em-leading-8 em-text-base s-justify\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"It is by great fortune that one comes to Kṛṣṇa consciousness on the path of bhakti-yoga to become well situated according to the Vedic direction. The ideal yogī concentrates his attention on Kṛṣṇa, who is called Śyāmasundara, who is as beautifully colored as a cloud, whose lotuslike face is as effulgent as the sun, whose dress is brilliant with jewels, and whose body is flower-garlanded. Illuminating all sides is His gorgeous luster, which is called the brahma-jyotir. He incarnates in different forms such as Rāma, Nṛsiṁha, Varāha and Kṛṣṇa, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, and He descends like a human being, as the son of mother Yaśodā, and He is known as Kṛṣṇa, Govinda and Vāsudeva. He is the perfect child, husband, friend and master, and He is full with all opulences and transcendental qualities. If one remains fully conscious of these features of the Lord, he is called the highest yogī.\"}}]}]\n36:[\"$\",\"$L2a\",\"child-2076\",{\"child\":\"$23:props:children:0:props:children:props:page:child_items:4:children:6\",\"hasId\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"em-mb-4 em-leading-8 em-text-base s-justify\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"This stage of highest perfection in yoga can be attained only by bhakti-yoga, as is confirmed in all Vedic literature:\"}}]}]\n37:[\"$\",\"$L2a\",\"child-2077\",{\"child\":\"$23:props:children:0:props:children:props:page:child_items:4:children:7\",\"hasId\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"em-mb-4 em-leading-8 em-text-base text-center italic\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"yasya deve parā bhaktir
yathā deve tathā gurau
tasyaite kathitā hy arthāḥ
prakāśante mahātmanaḥ\"}}]}]\n38:[\"$\",\"$L2a\",\"child-2078\",{\"child\":\"$23:props:children:0:props:children:props:page:child_items:4:children:8\",\"hasId\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"em-mb-4 em-leading-8 em-text-base s-justify\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"“Only unto those great souls who have implicit faith in both the Lord and the spiritual master are all the imports of Vedic knowledge automatically revealed.” (Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad 6.23)\"}}]}]\n39:[\"$\",\"$L2a\",\"child-2079\",{\"child\":\"$23:props:children:0:props:children:props:page:child_items:4:children:9\",\"hasId\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"em-mb-4 em-leading-8 em-text-base s-justify\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"Bhaktir asya bhajanaṁ tad ihāmutropādhi-nairāsyenāmuṣmin manaḥ-kalpanam, etad eva naiṣkarmyam. “Bhakti means devotional service to the Lord which is free from desire for material profit, either in this life or in the next. Devoid of such inclinations, one should fully absorb the mind in the Supreme. That is the purpose of naiṣkarmya.” (Gopāla-tāpanī Upaniṣad 1.15)\"}}]}]\n3a:[\"$\",\"$L2a\",\"child-2080\",{\"child\":\"$23:props:children:0:props:children:props:page:child_items:4:children:10\",\"hasId\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"em-mb-4 em-leading-8 em-text-base s-justify\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"These are some of the means for performance of bhakti, or Kṛṣṇa consciousness, the highest perfectional stage of the yoga system.\"}}]}]\n3b:[\"$\",\"$L2a\",\"child-2081\",{\"child\":\"$23:props:children:0:props:children:props:page:child_items:4:children:11\",\"hasId\":true,\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"em-mb-4 em-leading-8 em-text-base s-justify italic\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"Thus end the Bhaktivedanta Purports to"])self.__next_f.push([1," the Sixth Chapter of the Śrīmad Bhagavad-gītā in the matter of Dhyāna-yoga.\"}}]}]\n"])(fun